            <ArticleTitle>Role of loco-regional anaesthesia in preventing post-operative pain in major abdominal surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>89-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Loco-Regional Anaesthesia (LRA) is considered a valid anaesthesiological option in both operative and post-operative time because more safety than General Anaesthesia (GA), reducing perioperative side effects often linked to unsatisfactory analgesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We studied 72 pts who underwent abdominal surgery, randomized into two group of 36 subjects. Group 1 received GA and Group 2 received LRA, by peridural technique, and in both group the post-operative analgesia was carried out by a 24 hours elastomer. For pain examination a NRS numeric scale was used.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS AND CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The score of NRS scale was significantly lower in the LRA group than in GA group, thus confirming the LRA safety and effectiveness in reducing pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
